{
  "note": "Movement of Ions Across the Membrane",
  "source": "b97cc6a0a205",
  "script": "155194c56c5d",
  "voice": "Jessica Gallagher - Clear and Neutral",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "fee4ea4eb748",
  "duration": 205.035,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Movement of Ions Across the Membrane",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Ions have three ways of getting across the cell membrane.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 3.6
        },
        {
          "text": "These are passive diffusion through ion channels, facilitated diffusion, and active transport.",
          "start": 3.72,
          "end": 10.08
        },
        {
          "text": "For each one, we'll see how it works, how fast it goes, whether it costs energy and what it's for, before comparing all three.",
          "start": 10.56,
          "end": 18.88
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Passive diffusion through ion channels",
      "start": 19.722,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Let's begin with passive diffusion through ion channels.",
          "start": 19.722,
          "end": 23.522
        },
        {
          "text": "Here, ions flow through the pore of an open channel, strictly down their electrochemical gradient.",
          "start": 23.562,
          "end": 29.762
        },
        {
          "text": "That gradient is the combined pull of the concentration gradient and the electrical gradient.",
          "start": 29.802,
          "end": 35.202
        },
        {
          "text": "At no point is any energy consumed.",
          "start": 35.242,
          "end": 38.335
        },
        {
          "text": "The channel's gate simply opens in response to voltage, a ligand or mechanical stretch, and ions flood through at millions per second.",
          "start": 38.389,
          "end": 47.682
        },
        {
          "text": "The purpose of this route is rapid signalling.",
          "start": 47.722,
          "end": 50.562
        },
        {
          "text": "It's what generates the electrical currents underlying action potentials and synaptic potentials.",
          "start": 50.602,
          "end": 56.175
        },
        {
          "text": "The same route produces the resting potassium leak, which sets the resting membrane potential.",
          "start": 56.229,
          "end": 61.722
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Facilitated diffusion",
      "start": 62.485,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Next comes facilitated diffusion.",
          "start": 62.485,
          "end": 65.365
        },
        {
          "text": "Like the channel route, it is passive and runs down the gradient, with no adenosine triphosphate, or A T P, involved.",
          "start": 65.485,
          "end": 73.565
        },
        {
          "text": "This time, though, the ion or solute crosses via a carrier protein rather than an open pore.",
          "start": 73.605,
          "end": 79.965
        },
        {
          "text": "The carrier binds the molecule on one side, changes shape, and releases it on the other.",
          "start": 80.005,
          "end": 85.872
        },
        {
          "text": "Because of that shape change on every cycle, it's far slower than a channel.",
          "start": 85.978,
          "end": 90.605
        },
        {
          "text": "What's more, it can be saturated when all the carriers are occupied.",
          "start": 90.645,
          "end": 94.845
        },
        {
          "text": "Its purpose is to let charged or polar substances cross the membrane down their gradient in a controlled, selective way.",
          "start": 94.885,
          "end": 102.778
        },
        {
          "text": "It fills this role when a simple pore isn't the route used.",
          "start": 102.832,
          "end": 106.645
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Active transport",
      "start": 107.416,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Now for active transport, the only mechanism that moves ions against their electrochemical gradient.",
          "start": 107.416,
          "end": 114.376
        },
        {
          "text": "It's also the only one that costs energy.",
          "start": 114.456,
          "end": 117.616
        },
        {
          "text": "In primary active transport, a pump burns A T P directly.",
          "start": 117.656,
          "end": 122.429
        },
        {
          "text": "The sodium-potassium adenosine triphosphatase works this way, ejecting three sodium ions and importing two potassium ions per cycle.",
          "start": 122.483,
          "end": 131.696
        },
        {
          "text": "In secondary active transport, by contrast, the downhill flow of one ion is harnessed to drag another one uphill.",
          "start": 131.736,
          "end": 139.536
        },
        {
          "text": "That happens in the sodium-calcium exchanger, where three sodium ions entering the cell drive one calcium ion out.",
          "start": 139.576,
          "end": 147.656
        },
        {
          "text": "Per protein, active transport is the slowest of the three, yet it runs continuously.",
          "start": 147.736,
          "end": 153.376
        },
        {
          "text": "Its purpose is to build and maintain the ion gradients, so that sodium and calcium stay low inside the cell and potassium stays high inside.",
          "start": 153.416,
          "end": 163.056
        },
        {
          "text": "It restores those gradients after activity, too.",
          "start": 163.096,
          "end": 166.256
        },
        {
          "text": "In this way, it makes all passive signalling possible in the first place.",
          "start": 166.296,
          "end": 170.856
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Main differences",
      "start": 171.624,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "So, how do the three compare?",
          "start": 171.624,
          "end": 174.344
        },
        {
          "text": "Channels and facilitated diffusion both dissipate gradients passively.",
          "start": 174.624,
          "end": 179.104
        },
        {
          "text": "The only difference between them is the protein used, an open pore in one case and a carrier that changes shape in the other.",
          "start": 179.144,
          "end": 187.024
        },
        {
          "text": "That's why channels are fast and carriers are slower.",
          "start": 187.064,
          "end": 190.944
        },
        {
          "text": "Active transport, on the other hand, is the slow, energy-dependent builder of those very same gradients.",
          "start": 190.984,
          "end": 197.624
        },
        {
          "text": "And that's how ions move across the membrane, with two routes that run gradients down and one that pays to build them.",
          "start": 197.704,
          "end": 205.064
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
