    {"duration":44.930612,"alignment":{"characters":"Next comes facilitated diffusion. Like the channel route, it is passive and runs down the gradient, with no adenosine triphosphate, or A T P, involved. This time, though, the ion or solute crosses via a carrier protein rather than an open pore. The carrier binds the molecule on one side, changes shape, and releases it on the other. Because of that shape change on every cycle, it's far slower than a channel. What's more, it can be saturated when all the carriers are occupied. Its purpose is to let charged or polar substances cross the membrane down their gradient in a controlled, selective way. It fills this role when a simple pore isn't the route used.","starts":[0,100,200,300,400,480,544,608,672,736,800,880,960,1040,1120,1180,1240,1300,1360,1420,1480,1540,1600,1680,1740,1800,1860,1920,2000,2080,2160,2240,2320,2880,3000,3030,3060,3090,3120,3200,3227,3253,3280,3440,3463,3486,3509,3531,3554,3577,3600,3680,3728,3776,3824,3872,3920,4080,4120,4140,4160,4240,4320,4400,4480,4540,4600,4660,4720,4827,4933,5040,5120,5173,5227,5280,5440,5480,5520,5560,5600,5720,5750,5780,5810,5840,5880,5893,5907,5920,6200,6235,6270,6305,6340,6375,6410,6445,6480,6640,6760,6790,6820,6850,6880,7000,7060,7120,7200,7260,7320,7380,7440,7520,7600,7653,7707,7760,7800,7820,7840,7947,8053,8160,8240,8320,8400,8480,8560,8640,8720,8760,8800,8880,8960,9040,9120,9200,9360,9440,9760,9800,9840,9920,10000,10080,10272,10464,10656,10848,11040,11080,11120,11160,11200,11240,11280,11400,11430,11460,11490,11520,11560,11600,11667,11733,11800,11867,11933,12000,12053,12107,12124,12142,12160,12320,12400,12480,12560,12640,12720,12800,12880,13080,13120,13160,13200,13240,13280,13360,13408,13456,13504,13552,13600,13720,13840,13960,14000,14040,14080,14240,14280,14320,14377,14434,14491,14549,14606,14663,14720,14800,14891,14983,15074,15166,15257,15349,15440,15520,15573,15627,15680,15733,15787,15840,15920,15940,15960,15980,16000,16080,16120,16160,16240,16300,16360,16420,16480,