    {"duration":64.20898,"alignment":{"characters":"Now for active transport, the only mechanism that moves ions against their electrochemical gradient. It's also the only one that costs energy. In primary active transport, a pump burns A T P directly. The sodium-potassium adenosine triphosphatase works this way, ejecting three sodium ions and importing two potassium ions per cycle. In secondary active transport, by contrast, the downhill flow of one ion is harnessed to drag another one uphill. That happens in the sodium-calcium exchanger, where three sodium ions entering the cell drive one calcium ion out. Per protein, active transport is the slowest of the three, yet it runs continuously. Its purpose is to build and maintain the ion gradients, so that sodium and calcium stay low inside the cell and potassium stays high inside. It restores those gradients after activity, too. In this way, it makes all passive signalling possible in the first place.","starts":[0,80,160,240,400,480,560,640,840,873,907,940,973,1007,1040,1360,1396,1431,1467,1502,1538,1573,1609,1644,1680,2000,2080,2107,2133,2160,2240,2320,2400,2480,2560,2640,2680,2720,2760,2800,2840,2880,2987,3093,3200,3280,3300,3320,3340,3360,3440,3504,3568,3632,3696,3760,3840,3940,4040,4140,4240,4520,4560,4600,4640,4680,4720,4760,4800,4880,4912,4944,4976,5008,5040,5200,5232,5264,5296,5328,5360,5440,5520,5570,5620,5670,5720,5770,5820,5870,5920,6000,6070,6140,6210,6280,6350,6420,6490,6560,6960,7040,7080,7120,7200,7280,7360,7440,7520,7600,7680,7760,7813,7867,7920,8000,8060,8120,8180,8240,8320,8373,8427,8480,8560,8600,8640,8680,8720,8800,8880,8960,9040,9120,9200,9280,9427,9573,9720,9867,10013,10160,10200,10240,10320,10400,10480,10549,10617,10686,10754,10823,10891,10960,11040,11080,11120,11160,11200,11240,11280,11360,11440,11520,11600,11680,11760,11840,11920,12000,12080,12120,12160,12320,12360,12400,12507,12613,12720,12880,12920,12960,13000,13040,13120,13200,13360,13440,13520,13600,13840,14080,14120,14160,14200,14240,14280